<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- Every screen is served from the backend; there is no offline-only mode, only an offline
         copy of the library. Cleartext is permitted to a dev server in the DEBUG variant only
         (src/debug/res/xml/network_security_config.xml); production is HTTPS. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <!-- `SyncCenter.startMonitoring` reads `ConnectivityManager.getActiveNetwork` and registers a
         default-network callback; both are `@RequiresPermission(ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE)` and throw
         SecurityException without it. It has only ever worked because the merged manifest picked
         the permission up transitively from a dependency — a version bump that dropped it would
         silently break the app root. Declared here, where it is actually used.
         (iOS needs no entitlement for NWPathMonitor, so there is no counterpart on that side.) -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <!-- EPISODE ALERTS (`notifications/`).

         `POST_NOTIFICATIONS` is a runtime permission from API 33 and harmless below it; the ASK is
         raised contextually, from the Search tab's primer after an airing show has been added, the
         same moment iOS asks. Without the declaration `checkSelfPermission` answers DENIED forever
         and the feature is dead with nothing in the log to say why.

         `SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM`, and deliberately **never `USE_EXACT_ALARM`**: Play policy reserves
         that one for apps whose core function is alarms, timers or calendars, and declaring it here
         is a review rejection (research/product-decisions.md §4). This grant is DENIED BY DEFAULT on
         Android 14+ at targetSdk 36, so the inexact branch is the normal path and the app has to be
         correct on it — see `AlarmScheduler`, which ships both.

         `RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED` because the platform clears every alarm on reboot. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" />

    <!-- WHY THE WIDGET'S ART IS BLANK WITHOUT THIS, ON EXACTLY THE DEVICES THAT MATTER.

         `WidgetArt.grantToLaunchers` hands the launcher a per-URI read grant for the cached banner,
         and to do that it must first ASK WHO THE LAUNCHERS ARE — `queryIntentActivities` for
         MAIN/HOME. From Android 11 that query is subject to package-visibility filtering: an app
         that has not declared what it needs to see gets an EMPTY list back, with no error and no
         exception. The grant loop then runs zero times, the launcher opens the `content://` URI it
         was handed, and the platform refuses it:

             ContentProviderHelper: Permission Denial: opening provider
             androidx.core.content.FileProvider from …/nexuslauncher … not exported from UID …
             Icon: Unable to load image from URI: content://com.anitrack.app.fileprovider/…

         The card still draws — pill, title, episode, progress — so nothing looks broken; the art is
         simply missing, and only on API 30+. It rendered correctly on the API 26 floor throughout,
         which is the trap: the platform where this works is the one nobody ships to.

         **This must stay a narrow `<intent>` filter and must never become `QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES`** —
         that permission is Play-policy restricted in the same way `USE_EXACT_ALARM` is, and the app
         does not qualify for it. All this declares is "I need to see home screens", which is
         exactly and only what issuing the grant requires. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.HOME" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <application
        android:name=".PreviouslyApp"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Previously">

        <!--
          Four things here are load-bearing, and three of them fail silently if dropped.

          * `screenOrientation="portrait"` — there is no landscape layout anywhere in this app, on
            either platform (iOS pins `UISupportedInterfaceOrientations` to portrait).
          * `launchMode="singleTop"` — WITHOUT IT THE ALERT-TAP ROUTE SILENTLY DIES. With the
            default `standard` mode a notification tap on a WARM app creates a second MainActivity
            instance, `onNewIntent` never fires, and nothing happens — while a cold-start test still
            passes, because a cold start reads `onCreate`'s intent. (Not `singleTask`, which would
            additionally clear the tab stacks on every tap.)
          * `Theme.Previously.Splash` — the launcher theme is the splash theme, so the system's own
            splash sits on the same true black the ident's base does; `installSplashScreen()` swaps
            in `postSplashScreenTheme` (`Theme.Previously`) as it leaves.
          * NO `android:configChanges`. A font-scale change RECREATES the Activity by design, and
            the navigation stacks and each screen's state survive it because they are saveable —
            not because recreation was suppressed. Suppressing it would leave stale text metrics
            behind and make the accessibility half of the capture matrix un-photographable.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:screenOrientation="portrait"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Previously.Splash">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          EPISODE ALERTS (`notifications/`).

          * `AiringReceiver` is `exported="false"`: the alarm `PendingIntent` names it explicitly, so
            it needs no intent-filter and nothing outside this app has any business firing it. One
            alarm per air-time MINUTE wakes it, it posts every alert in that bucket as one designed
            bundle, and it re-arms. **An explicit-component PendingIntent at an undeclared receiver
            arms happily and fires into nothing**, which is why `AlarmScheduler` logs an error at its
            first arm if this element is missing — the manifest is the one part of that chain the
            compiler does not check.
          * `AlarmRearmReceiver` is `exported="true"` because the SYSTEM delivers its five broadcasts.
            Alarms survive neither a reboot, an app update, nor a clock change, and the exact-alarm
            grant can flip while the app is not running; each of those re-arms from the persisted
            plan, with no network and no session. Without it the armed set silently empties overnight
            while every bell in the UI still says armed.

          Neither is any use on a force-stopped app — Samsung's "Put unused apps to sleep" is on by
          default and Xiaomi denies Autostart — which receives no broadcasts at all. That is the
          feature's ceiling, and it is stated rather than mitigated.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".notifications.AiringReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <receiver
            android:name=".notifications.AlarmRearmReceiver"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.TIME_SET" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.TIMEZONE_CHANGED" />
            </intent-filter>
            <intent-filter>
                <!-- API 31+: the user granted or revoked "Alarms & reminders". -->
                <action android:name="android.app.action.SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM_PERMISSION_STATE_CHANGED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!--
          THE "UP NEXT" HOME-SCREEN WIDGET (`widget/`).

          Three things here fail silently if they are dropped, and one of them is invisible in QA
          until a launcher restarts.

          * The provider is `exported="true"` because the LAUNCHER — a different application — binds
            to it. It carries no data of its own; everything it renders it reads from this app's own
            files inside this app's own process.
          * `FileProvider` with `grantUriPermissions="true"` is what lets the widget hand the
            launcher a `content://` reference to a cached banner instead of a decoded bitmap. A
            bitmap crosses the Binder transaction and is charged against the platform's per-widget
            memory ceiling; a reference is free. **Without the provider — or without the explicit
            per-URI grant `WidgetArt` issues — the art is simply BLANK on most launchers, with no
            error anywhere**, and without the persistable half of that grant it goes blank again the
            next time the launcher process restarts. Its authority is
            `${applicationId}.fileprovider`, built by hand in `WidgetArt.authority`; the two must
            move together.
          * `WidgetBoundaryReceiver` is `exported="false"`: nothing outside this app has any business
            firing it. It is woken by ONE inexact alarm at the next episode's air instant — the only
            moment the card's meaning changes while nobody is touching the phone. **No exact-alarm
            permission is declared and none may be**: Play policy reserves `USE_EXACT_ALARM` for
            alarm, timer and calendar apps, and a five-minute window is indistinguishable to somebody
            reading a card that says "in 3h 12m".
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".widget.UpNextWidgetReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/widget_up_next_label">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/up_next_widget_info" />
        </receiver>

        <receiver
            android:name=".widget.WidgetBoundaryReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/file_paths" />
        </provider>
    </application>
</manifest>
